
// draft
padded(items: Int[], fillvalue: Int, n: Int, next_multiple: Bool) -> Int[]
	note "emit the items in order, then copies of fillvalue, until at least n elements are out, or, with next_multiple, until the count is a multiple of n"
	# The output begins with the items, in their order and with nothing left out.
	out[0 .. len(items)] == items
	# Every output element after the items is the fillvalue.
	all x in out[len(items) .. len(out)]: x == fillvalue
	# If next_multiple is false, the output's length is the larger of n and the number of items.
	not next_multiple -> len(out) == max(n, len(items))
	# If next_multiple is true and n is at least 1, the output has at least as many elements as the
	# items and fewer than the number of items plus n.
	next_multiple and n >= 1 -> len(out) >= len(items) and len(out) < len(items) + n
	# If next_multiple is true and n is at least 1, the output's length is a multiple of n (a
	# sibling obligation decides what a multiple is).
	next_multiple and n >= 1 -> multiple_of(len(out), n)
	// n of None (fillvalue emitted indefinitely) gives an infinite output and is not modelled; n is
	// a plain Int here
	// example wanted: items [1, 2, 3], fillvalue 0, n 5, next_multiple false: the docstring's first
	// case, padding up to n
	// example wanted: items [1, 2, 3, 4], fillvalue 0, n 3, next_multiple true: the docstring's
	// second case, padding up to the next multiple
	// example wanted: items [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8], n 5, next_multiple false: more items than n,
	// so nothing is appended and nothing is cut
	// example wanted: items of exactly n elements, next_multiple false: the threshold itself, so no
	// fill
	// example wanted: empty items, n 3, next_multiple false: the output is only fill, n copies
	// example wanted: empty items, n 3, next_multiple true: zero is already a multiple of n
	// example wanted: items whose length is already a multiple of n, next_multiple true: no fill is
	// added
	// example wanted: n of 0 (and a negative n), with next_multiple true and with it false: the
	// library raises here, so the author decides whether this becomes an error case in a Result
